{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgrsbZqFPyhAqaWnG7EUR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SgcoYKfp18UPKZ9AmyibDa","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.preact-adapter-06-prepare-0-1-0-release/README.md","old_string":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`","new_string":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/preact`\n\nUse a microtags component inside a Preact app, rendered on the server\nand hydrated in the browser, or rendered client-only. `toPreact(El,\n{ events })` takes an element class and returns a Preact component that\nrenders the host tag. Preact owns the host and its attributes, and the\nelement owns the host's children. See\n[ADR-010](./docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { toPreact } from 'microtags/preact'\nimport { NumberInput } from './number-input.js'\n\n// callback prop name -> DOM event name\nexport const PreactNumberInput = toPreact(NumberInput, {\n    events: { onChange: 'number-input:change' },\n})\n```\n\nThe component takes the element's declared props (typed from the\nclass), one callback prop per entry in `events`, and `class`, `id`,\n`aria-*` and `data-*` props, which pass through to the host.\n\n**Server render, then hydrate.** On the server, the adapter fills the\nhost with the component's own markup, so `renderToString` gives the\nsame HTML as the component's `render` static. Server code must import\na variant of the component that keeps its `.render` link; a class with\nno render link throws an error naming its tag.\n\n```ts\n// server\nimport { h } from 'preact'\nimport { renderToString } from 'preact-render-to-string'\n\nconst body = renderToString(h(App, null))\n```\n\n```ts\n// browser\nimport { h, hydrate } from 'preact'\n\nhydrate(h(App, null), document.getElementById('root')!)\n```\n\nIn `App`, use the component like any other:\n\n```ts\nh(PreactNumberInput, {\n    min: 1,\n    class: 'qty',\n    onChange: (ev:CustomEvent) => setQty(ev.detail),\n})\n```\n\nIn the browser, the host gets a fixed, empty `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\nWhile hydrating, Preact leaves that element's children alone, so the\nelement adopts the server markup. Because the value never changes,\nlater renders never write `innerHTML` either.\n\n**Client-only render.** With no server markup, call Preact's `render`.\nThe first render empties the host once, and the element renders itself\nwhen it connects, so the browser bundle needs the variant of the\ncomponent that keeps its render link.\n\n```ts\nimport { h, render } from 'preact'\n\nrender(h(PreactNumberInput, { min: 1 }), document.body)\n```\n\n**Two builds.** The import resolves by export condition, the same way\nas [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm). The `browser` condition selects a\nbuild that contains no render code. The default build, for Node and\nSSR, imports [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support).\n\n**Peer dependency.** `preact` is an optional peer dependency. Install\nit yourself. Only `microtags/preact` imports it.\n\n**No children.** The adapter does not accept Preact children. Passing\nthem is a type error, and the adapter throws.\n\n**Props before definition.** If the element is not defined yet when\nPreact first sets a prop, the element has no property by that name, so\nPreact sets an attribute instead. The component reads its declared\nprops from attributes when it connects, so string-valued props still\narrive. The adapter does not work around this.\n\n**Other host renderers.** The adapter is built on the\n[`renderParts`](#builderrenderfn-options) static, which returns the\nhost tag, its serialized attributes and its inner HTML separately. An\nadapter for another framework can build on the same parts.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":76132,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd578c50-847c-417e-9dc9-883d9c7ecbfc","uuid":"69c2f81e-a87b-4bd7-80f9-9ea9d443559a","timestamp":"2026-11-09T06:22:41.564Z","request_id":"req_011CgrsbZcrZUfrpxbjuYWnj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SgcoYKfp18UPKZ9AmyibDa":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.preact-adapter-06-prepare-0-1-0-release/README.md","old_string":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`","new_string":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/preact`\n\nUse a microtags component inside a Preact app, rendered on the server\nand hydrated in the browser, or rendered client-only. `toPreact(El,\n{ events })` takes an element class and returns a Preact component that\nrenders the host tag. Preact owns the host and its attributes, and the\nelement owns the host's children. See\n[ADR-010](./docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { toPreact } from 'microtags/preact'\nimport { NumberInput } from './number-input.js'\n\n// callback prop name -> DOM event name\nexport const PreactNumberInput = toPreact(NumberInput, {\n    events: { onChange: 'number-input:change' },\n})\n```\n\nThe component takes the element's declared props (typed from the\nclass), one callback prop per entry in `events`, and `class`, `id`,\n`aria-*` and `data-*` props, which pass through to the host.\n\n**Server render, then hydrate.** On the server, the adapter fills the\nhost with the component's own markup, so `renderToString` gives the\nsame HTML as the component's `render` static. Server code must import\na variant of the component that keeps its `.render` link; a class with\nno render link throws an error naming its tag.\n\n```ts\n// server\nimport { h } from 'preact'\nimport { renderToString } from 'preact-render-to-string'\n\nconst body = renderToString(h(App, null))\n```\n\n```ts\n// browser\nimport { h, hydrate } from 'preact'\n\nhydrate(h(App, null), document.getElementById('root')!)\n```\n\nIn `App`, use the component like any other:\n\n```ts\nh(PreactNumberInput, {\n    min: 1,\n    class: 'qty',\n    onChange: (ev:CustomEvent) => setQty(ev.detail),\n})\n```\n\nIn the browser, the host gets a fixed, empty `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\nWhile hydrating, Preact leaves that element's children alone, so the\nelement adopts the server markup. Because the value never changes,\nlater renders never write `innerHTML` either.\n\n**Client-only render.** With no server markup, call Preact's `render`.\nThe first render empties the host once, and the element renders itself\nwhen it connects, so the browser bundle needs the variant of the\ncomponent that keeps its render link.\n\n```ts\nimport { h, render } from 'preact'\n\nrender(h(PreactNumberInput, { min: 1 }), document.body)\n```\n\n**Two builds.** The import resolves by export condition, the same way\nas [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm). The `browser` condition selects a\nbuild that contains no render code. The default build, for Node and\nSSR, imports [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support).\n\n**Peer dependency.** `preact` is an optional peer dependency. Install\nit yourself. Only `microtags/preact` imports it.\n\n**No children.** The adapter does not accept Preact children. Passing\nthem is a type error, and the adapter throws.\n\n**Props before definition.** If the element is not defined yet when\nPreact first sets a prop, the element has no property by that name, so\nPreact sets an attribute instead. The component reads its declared\nprops from attributes when it connects, so string-valued props still\narrive. The adapter does not work around this.\n\n**Other host renderers.** The adapter is built on the\n[`renderParts`](#builderrenderfn-options) static, which returns the\nhost tag, its serialized attributes and its inner HTML separately. An\nadapter for another framework can build on the same parts.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgcoYKfp18UPKZ9AmyibDa","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/nick/code/microtags.preact-adapter-06-prepare-0-1-0-release/README.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd578c50-847c-417e-9dc9-883d9c7ecbfc","uuid":"d0d9fe5c-7b4e-4300-9a8e-45216b1474c9","timestamp":"2026-11-09T06:22:41.620Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.preact-adapter-06-prepare-0-1-0-release/README.md","oldString":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`","newString":"[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/preact`\n\nUse a microtags component inside a Preact app, rendered on the server\nand hydrated in the browser, or rendered client-only. `toPreact(El,\n{ events })` takes an element class and returns a Preact component that\nrenders the host tag. Preact owns the host and its attributes, and the\nelement owns the host's children. See\n[ADR-010](./docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { toPreact } from 'microtags/preact'\nimport { NumberInput } from './number-input.js'\n\n// callback prop name -> DOM event name\nexport const PreactNumberInput = toPreact(NumberInput, {\n    events: { onChange: 'number-input:change' },\n})\n```\n\nThe component takes the element's declared props (typed from the\nclass), one callback prop per entry in `events`, and `class`, `id`,\n`aria-*` and `data-*` props, which pass through to the host.\n\n**Server render, then hydrate.** On the server, the adapter fills the\nhost with the component's own markup, so `renderToString` gives the\nsame HTML as the component's `render` static. Server code must import\na variant of the component that keeps its `.render` link; a class with\nno render link throws an error naming its tag.\n\n```ts\n// server\nimport { h } from 'preact'\nimport { renderToString } from 'preact-render-to-string'\n\nconst body = renderToString(h(App, null))\n```\n\n```ts\n// browser\nimport { h, hydrate } from 'preact'\n\nhydrate(h(App, null), document.getElementById('root')!)\n```\n\nIn `App`, use the component like any other:\n\n```ts\nh(PreactNumberInput, {\n    min: 1,\n    class: 'qty',\n    onChange: (ev:CustomEvent) => setQty(ev.detail),\n})\n```\n\nIn the browser, the host gets a fixed, empty `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\nWhile hydrating, Preact leaves that element's children alone, so the\nelement adopts the server markup. Because the value never changes,\nlater renders never write `innerHTML` either.\n\n**Client-only render.** With no server markup, call Preact's `render`.\nThe first render empties the host once, and the element renders itself\nwhen it connects, so the browser bundle needs the variant of the\ncomponent that keeps its render link.\n\n```ts\nimport { h, render } from 'preact'\n\nrender(h(PreactNumberInput, { min: 1 }), document.body)\n```\n\n**Two builds.** The import resolves by export condition, the same way\nas [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm). The `browser` condition selects a\nbuild that contains no render code. The default build, for Node and\nSSR, imports [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support).\n\n**Peer dependency.** `preact` is an optional peer dependency. Install\nit yourself. Only `microtags/preact` imports it.\n\n**No children.** The adapter does not accept Preact children. Passing\nthem is a type error, and the adapter throws.\n\n**Props before definition.** If the element is not defined yet when\nPreact first sets a prop, the element has no property by that name, so\nPreact sets an attribute instead. The component reads its declared\nprops from attributes when it connects, so string-valued props still\narrive. The adapter does not work around this.\n\n**Other host renderers.** The adapter is built on the\n[`renderParts`](#builderrenderfn-options) static, which returns the\nhost tag, its serialized attributes and its inner HTML separately. An\nadapter for another framework can build on the same parts.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`","originalFile":"# microtags\n\n[![tests](https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/nichoth/microtags/nodejs.yml?style=flat-square)](https://github.com/nichoth/microtags/actions/workflows/nodejs.yml)\n[![types](https://img.shields.io/npm/types/microtags?style=flat-square)](README.md)\n[![module](https://img.shields.io/badge/module-ESM%2FCJS-blue?style=flat-square)](README.md)\n[![semantic versioning](https://img.shields.io/badge/semver-2.0.0-blue?logo=semver&style=flat-square)](https://semver.org/)\n[![Common Changelog](https://nichoth.github.io/badge/common-changelog.svg)](./CHANGELOG.md)\n[![install size](https://flat.badgen.net/packagephobia/install/microtags?cache-control=no-cache)](https://packagephobia.com/result?p=microtags)\n[![gzip size](https://img.shields.io/bundlephobia/minzip/microtags?style=flat-square)](https://bundlephobia.com/package/microtags)\n[![license](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-Big_Time-blue?style=flat-square)](LICENSE)\n\nWeb component helper with reactive props, automatic cleanup, and\n[no Shadow DOM](https://gomakethings.com/the-shadow-dom-is-an-antipattern/).\nReactive via [`alien-signals`](https://github.com/stackblitz/alien-signals),\ninspired by [`nanotags`](https://github.com/psd-coder/nanotags).\n\nThis package adds some provisions for serverside rendering.\n\n| Entry | Size (min + brotli) |\n|-------|---------------------|\n| `microtags` | <!-- size -->4.01 KB<!-- /size --> |\n| `microtags/htm` | <!-- size:htm -->1.23 KB<!-- /size:htm --> |\n| `microtags/preact` | <!-- size:preact -->0.44 KB<!-- /size:preact --> |\n\n<details><summary><h2>Contents</h2></summary>\n\n<!-- toc -->\n\n- [Install](#install)\n  * [Import map](#import-map)\n  * [Pre-bundled](#pre-bundled)\n- [Get Started](#get-started)\n  * [1. Call `.define`](#1-call-define)\n  * [2. Call the `.with*` Methods](#2-call-the-with-methods)\n  * [3. Call `.setup`](#3-call-setup)\n- [Full Example](#full-example)\n- [Validation](#validation)\n  * [Prop validation](#prop-validation)\n    + [`p.schema` vs Standard Schema](#pschema-vs-standard-schema)\n  * [Client-side validation](#client-side-validation)\n- [Serverside Example](#serverside-example)\n  * [`.TAG`](#tag)\n  * [`.refs`](#refs)\n- [Hydration](#hydration)\n- [API](#api)\n  * [`define`](#define)\n    + [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder)\n  * [`builder.withProps`](#builderwithprops)\n    + [`p.json`](#pjson)\n    + [`p.schema`](#pschema)\n  * [`builder.withRefs(fn)`](#builderwithrefsfn)\n  * [`builder.withContexts`](#builderwithcontexts)\n    + [`.withContexts` Example](#withcontexts-example)\n  * [`builder.render(fn, options?)`](#builderrenderfn-options)\n  * [`builder.setup(ctx)`](#buildersetupctx)\n    + [`ctx`](#ctx)\n      - [`ctx` Methods](#ctx-methods)\n      - [`ctx.bind`](#ctxbind)\n  * [`bindProp`](#bindprop)\n- [Missing refs](#missing-refs)\n  * [Checking refs in tests](#checking-refs-in-tests)\n- [Typed Events](#typed-events)\n- [Templates](#templates)\n- [Subpath Exports](#subpath-exports)\n  * [`microtags/context`](#microtagscontext)\n  * [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender)\n    + [`renderList`](#renderlist)\n      - [`renderList` Example](#renderlist-example)\n    + [`render`](#render)\n      - [`render` Example](#render-example)\n  * [`microtags/util`](#microtagsutil)\n    + [Coercion](#coercion)\n    + [`runAll`](#runall)\n    + [`toAttributes`](#toattributes)\n  * [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm)\n    + [What escaping does not cover](#what-escaping-does-not-cover)\n  * [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support)\n  * [`microtags/build`](#microtagsbuild)\n  * [`microtags/esbuild`](#microtagsesbuild)\n  * [`microtags/vite`](#microtagsvite)\n  * [`microbuild`](#microbuild)\n  * [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep)\n- [Differences from `nanotags`](#differences-from-nanotags)\n  * [Reactivity primitive](#reactivity-primitive)\n  * [Effects + Subscriptions](#effects--subscriptions)\n  * [Refs: `r.all` instead of `r.many`](#refs-rall-instead-of-rmany)\n  * [Context API](#context-api)\n  * [Props](#props)\n  * [Not ported](#not-ported)\n  * [Additions / New Features](#additions--new-features)\n\n<!-- tocstop -->\n\n</details>\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nnpm i -S microtags\n```\n\n### Import map\n\nNo build step needed. Resolve `microtags` with an\n[import map](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script/type/importmap)\nand `esm.sh`.\n\n\n```html\n<script type=\"importmap\">\n{\n    \"imports\": {\n        \"microtags\": \"https://esm.sh/microtags\"\n    }\n}\n</script>\n\n<script type=\"module\">\n    import { define } from 'microtags'\n</script>\n```\n\nSubpaths follow the same URL pattern: `microtags/context`, `microtags/render`.\n\n### Pre-bundled\n\nThis package exposes `microtags/min`, which is a single self-contained file\nwith `alien-signals` inlined.\n\n```html\n<script type=\"importmap\">\n{\n    \"imports\": {\n        \"microtags\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/microtags/dist/index.min.js\"\n    }\n}\n</script>\n```\n\n## Get Started\n\n### 1. Call `.define`\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\n\nconst myCounter = define('my-counter')\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Call the `.with*` Methods\n\nMethods `withProps`, `withRefs`, and `withContexts` are optional and can appear\nin any order. `.setup` ends the chain. It calls `customElements.define` under\nthe hood and returns a typed constructor.\n\n```ts\nmyCounter\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),  // the starting count (an attribute)\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({  // keys in this object are the `data-ref` names in HTML\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),  // typed as HTMLDivElement,\n        inc: r.one(),  // depends on a child in HTML with `data-ref=\"inc\"`\n        dec: r.one(),  // depends on a child in HTML with `data-ref=\"dec\"`\n        copy: r.one(),  // the <copy-btn> element, via `data-ref=\"copy\"`\n    }))\n```\n\n### 3. Call `.setup`\n\nMethod `.setup` is the terminal step in the method chain. It takes a function\nthat gets called with a `context` object. This is where \"hydration\" happens.\n\n```ts\nmyCounter\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        const count = signal(ctx.props.start())\n        // `ctx.refs` are expected to be HTML elements with a matching `data-ref`\n        const display = ctx.refs.display\n\n        // whenever `count` changes\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            display.textContent = `Count: ${count()}`\n            display.style.color = count() < 0 ? 'red' : 'inherit'\n        })\n\n        // Reflect the live count onto the copy button's `value` attribute.\n        // Because `value` is attribute-backed, this flows into <copy-btn>'s\n        // own signal, so it always copies the current count.\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            const copy = ctx.refs.copy\n            copy.setAttribute('value', String(count()))\n        })\n\n        /**\n         * Listen for clicks.\n         */\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.inc, 'click', () => {\n            count(count() + 1)\n        })\n\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.dec, 'click', () => {\n            count(count() - 1)\n        })\n    })\n```\n\n## Full Example\n\nThis example expects the full HTML to already be in the DOM.\nDefining the components \"hydrates\" the page (adds behavior).\n\nIf you want to client-side render the components, that is possible too.\nYou would update the DOM inside `.setup`.\n\n>\n> [!NOTE]  \n> The server should know how to create the markup that components depend\n> on here. See [the serverside example](#serverside-example).\n>\n\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { signal } from 'alien-signals'\n\n/**\n *  - attribute-backed prop (`start`)\n *  - data-ref elements -- each needs a data-ref attribute, eg data-ref=\"inc\"\n *  - ctx.effect, ctx.on\n */\nexport const myCounter = define('my-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({  // the keys in this object are the ref names\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),  // typed as HTMLDivElement\n        inc: r.one(),  // defaults to HTMLElement\n        dec: r.one(),\n        copy: r.one(),\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // all event <-> state logic here\n        const count = signal(ctx.props.start())\n        const display = ctx.refs.display\n\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            display.textContent = `Count: ${count()}`\n            display.style.color = count() < 0 ? 'red' : 'inherit'\n        })\n\n        // Reflect the live count onto the copy button's `value` attribute.\n        // Because `value` is attribute-backed, this flows into <copy-btn>'s\n        // own signal, so it always copies the current count.\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            const copy = ctx.refs.copy\n            copy.setAttribute('value', String(count()))\n        })\n\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.inc, 'click', () => {\n            count(count() + 1)\n        })\n\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.dec, 'click', () => {\n            count(count() - 1)\n        })\n    })\n```\n\n```html\n<my-counter start=\"5\">\n    <div data-ref=\"display\"></div>\n    <button data-ref=\"dec\">-</button>\n    <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>\n</my-counter>\n```\n\n## Validation\n\nThere are two separate things people call \"validation\":\n\n1. **Prop validation** checks the attributes passed into a component.\n   Pass a [Standard Schema](https://standardschema.dev)\n   (Zod, Valibot, ArkType) to [`p.schema`](#withprops), and it coerces the\n   attribute value and falls back to `undefined` when the value doesn't coerce.\n2. **Form input validation** -- aka client-side validation -- checks live user\n   input in a form, for example a value that changes on every keystroke.\n   There is no dedicated API for this. Validate the schema directly\n   inside `.setup()` and hold the result as a signal.\n\n### Prop validation\n\nPass a [Standard Schema](https://standardschema.dev) validator to `p.schema`.\nThe validator runs against the raw attribute and its output becomes the\nsignal's value; anything it rejects falls back to `undefined`.\n\n```ts\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\n\n// Any Standard Schema validator works (Zod, Valibot, ArkType). microtags\n// only sees the shared `~standard` interface, so it has no validation\n// library of its own and no dependency on the one you pick.\nconst Variant = z.enum(['info', 'success', 'warning', 'danger'])\n\ndefine('status-badge')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        // Validates the raw `variant` attribute. A value outside the\n        // four options falls back to `undefined`.\n        variant: p.schema(Variant),\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            const variant = ctx.props.variant() ?? 'info'\n            ctx.host.className = `badge badge-${variant}`\n        })\n    })\n```\n\n```html\n<status-badge variant=\"success\">Saved</status-badge>\n\n<!-- \"purple\" is not in the enum, so ctx.props.variant() is undefined -->\n<status-badge variant=\"purple\">Unknown</status-badge>\n```\n\n#### `p.schema` vs Standard Schema\n\n[Standard Schema](https://standardschema.dev) is a small shared interface\nthat Zod, Valibot and ArkType all implement. `p.schema` validates through that\ninterface, so microtags ships no validator of its own.\n\nA schema prop is attribute-backed and observed, exactly like `p.string()` or\n`p.number()`: editing the attribute re-runs the validator and pushes the result\nto `ctx.props`.\n\nThe validator's input is the raw attribute string, or `null` when the attribute\nis absent. Because the input is always a string, schemas for non-string values\nmust coerce. Use `z.coerce.number()`, not `z.number()`:\n\n```ts\n.withProps(p => ({\n    // \"5\" -> 5; \"0\" and the absent attribute both fall back to undefined\n    quantity: p.schema(z.coerce.number().int().min(1)),\n}))\n```\n\n**On success the signal holds the validator's output value**; on failure it\nfalls back to `undefined`. The inferred signal type is the schema's output,\nso guard for the `undefined` case, or fold a default into the schema so it\nnever fails:\n\n\n```ts\n{\n    // always 'info' | 'success' | 'warning' | 'danger', never undefined\n    variant: p.schema(Variant.catch('info')),\n}\n```\n\nOnly synchronous schemas are supported; an async schema throws when the\nattribute is validated.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Client-side validation\n\nThere is no special API here. Validate input inside `.setup`, \n\nSee [example/subscribe-form.ts](./example/subscribe-form.ts).\n\n```ts\n// form input (client-side) validation\nimport { signal, startBatch, endBatch } from 'alien-signals'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\n\nconst Email = z.email('Please enter a valid email address.')\n\ndefine('subscribe-form')\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        form: r.one('form'),\n        input: r.one('input'),\n        submit: r.one('button'),\n        error: r.one('.error'),\n        status: r.one('.status'),\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        const value = signal('')\n        const touched = signal<boolean>(false)\n\n        // capture the live value (clears the field on reset)\n        ctx.bind(value, ctx.refs.input)\n\n        // only reveal errors after the first blur\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.input, 'blur', () => touched(true))\n\n        // ctx.effect is alien-signals/effect with automatic/correct disposal\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            // subscribe to `value` and `touched`\n            const result = Email.safeParse(value())\n            const message = (result.success ?\n                '' :\n                result.error.issues[0].message)\n            const showError = touched() && !result.success\n\n            ctx.refs.submit.disabled = !result.success\n            ctx.refs.input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(showError))\n            ctx.refs.error.textContent = showError ? message : ''\n        })\n\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.form, 'submit', ev => {\n            // subscribe to DOM events\n            ev.preventDefault()\n            if (!Email.safeParse(value()).success) return\n            ctx.refs.status.textContent = 'Thanks for subscribing!'\n            startBatch()\n            value('')\n            touched(false)\n            endBatch()\n        })\n    })\n```\n\nThe error stays hidden until the field is blurred once, then updates live,\nand the submit button is disabled until valid. See `example/subscribe-form.ts`.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n## Serverside Example\n\nSee [./example/ssr.ts](./example/ssr.ts) for an example of rendering an HTML\nstring and then \"hydrating\" the page on the client.\n\n> [!NOTE]  \n> The server must know how to render the HTML that the components depend on.\n\n### `.TAG`\n\nEach tag name is exposed as a property `.TAG`. You can import the web\ncomponents in Node, even though Node does not have browser APIs.\n\n```ts\nimport { MyCounter, CountBtn, CopyBtn } from './index.js'\n\n// this runs in Node\n\nexport function render ():string {\n    return `<div>\n        <${MyCounter.TAG} start=\"${START}\">\n            <div data-ref=\"${MyCounter.refs.display}\"\n                role=\"status\"\n                class=\"count\"\n            >${START}</div>\n\n            <div class=\"controls\">\n                <${CountBtn.TAG}\n                    class=\"dec\"\n                    data-ref=\"${MyCounter.refs.dec}\"\n                    aria-label=\"Decrement\"\n                >\n                    <button>-</button>\n                </${CountBtn.TAG}>\n\n                <${CopyBtn.TAG}\n                    class=\"copy\"\n                    data-ref=\"${MyCounter.refs.copy}\"\n                ></${CopyBtn.TAG}>\n\n                <${CountBtn.TAG}\n                    class=\"inc\"\n                    data-ref=\"${MyCounter.refs.inc}\"\n                    aria-label=\"Increment\"\n                >\n                    <button>+</button>\n                </${CountBtn.TAG}>\n            </div>\n        </${MyCounter.TAG}>\n    </div>`\n}\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Custom elements must always have a closing tag, even when empty, like\n> `<copy-btn></copy-btn>` above. HTML has no self-closing custom\n> elements. The browser parses `<copy-btn />` as an open tag and treats\n> everything after it as its children.\n\n### `.refs`\n\nWeb components created with `microtags` have a property `.refs`. It is an\nobject of ref names you passed in. Use it to create `data-ref` attributes.\n\n```ts\nimport { MyCounter } from '../example.js'\n\nexport function render () {\n    return `\n        <div\n            data-ref=\"${MyCounter.refs.display}\"\n            role=\"status\"\n            class=\"count\"\n        >${START}</div>\n    `\n}\n```\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Hydration\n\nIn the [preceding serverside example](#serverside-example), the\nserver must know how to render the HTML that the client components depend on.\nThat means we have an unspecified dependency between HTML and JS.\nHydration is one way to render the correct HTML.\n\nYou can write a `.render` method, and then use a build tool\nto strip the `.render` method from the JS sent to the client. That way\nthe clientside bundle stays small, but on the server, every component can\nrender itself, and the components shipped to the client deal with behavior only.\n\nSee [`example-hydration/`](./example-hydration/) for a working example that\nuses Vite, and more docs.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n## API\n\n### `define`\n\nFunction `define(tagName)` returns a [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder).\n\n```js\nfunction define (tagName:string):ComponentBuilder\n```\n\n\n#### `ComponentBuilder`\n\nThe object returned by [`define`](#define). Includes methods:\n\n| Method | Returns | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `.TAG` | `string` | The tag name passed to `define`. See [`.TAG`](#tag). |\n| `.withProps(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare reactive attributes. See [Props](#props). |\n| `.withRefs(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare DOM refs resolved once the element connects. See [Refs](#refs-1). |\n| `.withContexts(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Consume context values published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n| `.render(fn, opts?)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Attach a server-render function. See [`.render`](#builderrenderfn-options) below. Not to be confused with the DOM helper [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender). |\n| `.setup(fn)` | `MicrotagElementClass` | Registers the custom element and returns its class. See [`setup`](#setup) below. |\n\nMethods `withProps`, `withRefs`, and `withContexts` each return a new builder,\nso they chain in any order; `setup` ends the chain by registering the element.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n### `builder.withProps`\n\nDeclare reactive attributes via `withProps`. Each prop becomes:\n\n* An observed HTML attribute (auto-synced via `attributeChangedCallback`)\n* A signal at `ctx.props.propName`\n* Four built-in coercers turn raw attribute strings into typed values:\n\n| Coercer | Attribute present | Attribute absent |\n|---------|-------------------|------------------|\n| `p.string()` | the raw string | `\"\"` |\n| `p.number()` | `Number(val)`, so `NaN` if not numeric | `NaN` |\n| `p.boolean()` | `true`, whatever the value, including `\"\"` and `\"false\"` | `false` |\n| `p.json<T>()` | `JSON.parse(val)`, or `undefined` if it does not parse | `undefined` |\n\n`p.boolean()` follows HTML boolean-attribute semantics: presence means\n`true`, like `disabled` or `checked`. To turn a boolean prop off, remove the\nattribute rather than setting it to `\"false\"`.\n\n```ts\n.withProps(p => ({\n    count:   p.number(),           // attribute -> number  (NaN when absent)\n    label:   p.string(),           // attribute -> string  ('' when absent)\n    active:  p.boolean(),          // attribute -> boolean (true if present)\n    data:    p.json<MyType>(),     // attribute -> JSON.parse or undefined\n    schema:  p.schema(zodSchema),  // Standard Schema (Zod, Valibot, ArkType)\n    value:   p.prop(0),            // property-only, not an observed attribute\n}))\n```\n\nInside `.setup()`, every prop is a callable signal:\n\n```ts\nctx.props.count()      // read\nctx.props.count(42)    // write\n```\n\n\n#### `p.json`\n\n`p.json<T>()` -- a prop whose attribute value is parsed with\n`JSON.parse`. The type parameter `T` annotates the parsed result; it is a\ntype only, so the value is not validated at runtime. The resulting signal is\ntyped `T | undefined`.\n\n```ts\n.withProps(p => ({\n    user: p.json<{ id:number; name:string }>(),\n}))\n```\n\n```html\n<my-el user='{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Alice\"}'></my-el>\n```\n\nLike the string/number/boolean validators it is attribute-backed and\nobserved, so editing the attribute re-parses and pushes the new value to\n`ctx.props.user`. It falls back to `undefined` in two cases: when the\nattribute is absent, and when the value is not valid JSON. Parse errors are\nswallowed rather than thrown.\n\n\n```ts\n// check for `undefined` case\nctx.effect(() => {\n    const user = ctx.props.user()\n    if (!user) return\n    ctx.refs.name.textContent = user.name\n})\n```\n\n#### `p.schema`\n\nFor input you want validated rather than only parsed, pass a Standard Schema\nto [`p.schema`](#withprops) instead.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.withRefs(fn)`\n\nTakes a function that gets called with a helpful query selector, `r`.\n\nCall `r.one()` / `r.all()` with no argument to match `[data-ref=\"<key>\"]`,\nwhere `key` is the key name you passed in. Or pass a CSS selector to query by\nit instead. Queries are resolved relative to the host element, during\n[the `connectedCallback`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_custom_elements#custom_element_lifecycle_callbacks).\n\nAll refs returned from this callback are typed as much as possible, and exposed\non the `context` object passed to the `.setup` function.\n\n\n```ts\nclass ComponentBuilder {\n    withRefs<R extends Record<string, RefDef>> (\n        fn:(r:RefsDSL) => R\n    ):ComponentBuilder<Props, R, CtxDefs>\n}\n```\n\n\nTakes a function that gets called with a [`RefsDSL`](./src/builders.ts#L54)\nobject. It should return an object of child elements that you want to keep\nreferences to.\n\nThe `data-ref` attribute is a fallback. It's used when you don't pass a\nselector to `r.one()`. If you pass explicit CSS selectors to `withRefs`,\ne.g. `{ copy: r.one('button') }`, then the HTML does not require a `data-ref`\nattribute.\n\n\n```ts\n.withRefs(r => ({\n    button: r.one(),   // resolves [data-ref=\"button\"] (throws if missing)\n    items:  r.all(),   // resolves all [data-ref=\"items\"] (empty array if none)\n}))\n```\n\nIf there are no `data-ref` attribtues in the HTML, use CSS query selectors.\n\n```ts\n.withRefs(r => ({\n    code:  r.one('code'),    // host.querySelector('code')\n    items: r.all('li'),      // host.querySelectorAll('li')\n}))\n```\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.withContexts`\n\nContext is for the case where a parent component defines some state, e.g. a\n`theme`, and the top-level (application) knows nothing about the theme, but the\napplication *does* determine what children the parent renders\n(the app passes in children), and the children need to know the theme.\n\n```ts\nclass ComponentBuilder {\n    withContexts<C extends Record<string, ContextToken<unknown>>> (\n        fn:(c:ContextDSL) => C\n    ):ComponentBuilder<Props, RefDefs, C>\n}\n```\n\n#### `.withContexts` Example\n\n```html\n<!-- The application composes this markup. -->\n<theme-provider>\n    <some-child></some-child>\n    <another-child></another-child>\n</theme-provider>\n```\n\nIn that case, there is no way for the \"parent\" component to pass the theme to\nthe children. The root level (application) doesn't know what the theme is.\n\nContext gives us a way to model this. The context-provider\npublishes the value, and any descendants opt in with\n`withContexts`.\n\n>\n> [!IMPORTANT]  \n> Context is only relevant on the client-side.\n> Context gives you nothing for first paint or the no-JS case.\n>\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { createContext, provide } from 'microtags/context'\n\nconst ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n\n// Parent: owns the theme and publishes it to its descendants. It does\n// not reference the children below. The application composes those in.\ndefine('theme-provider')\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        const rmContext = provide(ctx.host, ThemeToken, 'dark')\n        ctx.onCleanup(rmContext)\n    })\n\n// Child: opts in to the theme and reads whichever provider it is\n// composed into. It never receives the value as a prop.\ndefine('themed-card')\n    .withContexts(() => ({\n        theme: ThemeToken,\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            ctx.host.dataset.theme = ctx.contexts.theme\n        })\n    })\n```\n\n```html\n<theme-provider>\n    <themed-card>Card content</themed-card>\n</theme-provider>\n```\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.render(fn, options?)`\n\nAttach a render function that produces the component's inner HTML as a\nstring. The render function receives a context with `props` (plain\nresolved values, not signals), `refs`, `TAG`, `children` (inner HTML\nfrom the caller), and `attrs` (extra attributes from the caller).\n\n```ts\ndefine('my-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one(),\n        dec: r.one(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs }) => {\n        return `\n            <div data-ref=\"${refs.display}\">\n                ${props.start}\n            </div>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.dec}\">-</button>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.inc}\">+</button>\n        `\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // hydration code only\n    })\n```\n\nThe returned element class gains three statics: `render(props,\noptions?)` which returns complete host markup, `renderParts(props,\noptions?)` which returns the same render as parts, and `props`, the list\nof declared prop names. `options` accepts `children` (a string of inner\nHTML) and `attrs` (an object of extra attributes, all strings).\n\n`renderParts` returns `{ tag, attrs, html }`: the host tag name, the\nhost attributes as serialized (not yet escaped) strings, and the inner\nHTML. `attrs` lists the declared props first, then the extra `attrs`,\nskipping prop-only props, null or false values, and extra names that are\nnot valid attribute names. A true boolean is the empty string. Use it to\nbuild the host with another renderer, such as a UI framework, while\n`render` stays the string form:\n\n```ts\nimport 'microtags/render-support'\nimport type { RenderParts } from 'microtags'\n\nconst { tag, attrs, html }:RenderParts = MyCounter.renderParts({\n    start: 5,\n})\n// tag: 'my-counter', attrs: { start: '5' }, html: '<div ...'\n```\n\n`props` are the names declared in `.withProps`. They are serialized onto\nthe host tag with the prop's serializer, so `.setup` can coerce them back\non the client. `attrs` are any other attributes the caller put on the\ntag, such as `class`, `id`, or `aria-*`. They are written to the host tag\nas-is, and are available to the render function so it can forward them\ninside. See [Hydration](#hydration) for an example.\n\nThe static `render` (and `renderParts`) needs the server render\nsupport module, which builds the host tag and serializes props. The `microtags/htm`\ntemplater loads it, on the server and in the browser. Code that calls\na component's `render` static directly, without the templater, must\nimport [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support) once.\nWithout it, `render` and `renderParts` throw an error naming that\nmodule.\n\n```ts\nimport 'microtags/render-support'\nimport { MyCounter } from './components.js'\n\nconst markup = MyCounter.render({ start: 5 })\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> This is a breaking change in 0.0.x. Before it, `render` worked with\n> no extra import.\n\nPass `{ client: true }` to keep the render function in the client\nbundle after [`microbuild`](#microbuild) strips it:\n\n```ts\n.render(renderFn, { client: true })\n```\n\nSee [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep) for when to\nuse each.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `.render` on the builder is unrelated to\n> [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender), which is a DOM reconciler\n> for lists and single items.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.setup(ctx)`\n\nRegisters the custom element via `customElements.define` and returns the\nelement class. The returned class exposes `.TAG` and `.refs` as static\nmembers, for use in [serverside rendering](#serverside-example).\n\n```ts\nclass ComponentBuilder {\n    setup (\n        fn:(ctx:SetupContext<\n            Props,\n            RefsMap<RefDefs>,\n            ContextsMap<CtxDefs>\n        >) => void\n    ):MicrotagElementClass<RefDefs> {\n}\n```\n\nThe callback function runs once per element instance, and is called with the\nfollowing `context` object.\n\nTeardown waits a microtask. When the element is removed, its cleanups\n(listeners, effects, bindings, `onCleanup` callbacks) run on the next\nmicrotask, and only if it is still disconnected then. So an element that is\nremoved and re-inserted in the same tick (a move) keeps its setup and\nstate, even in browsers without `moveBefore`. After a real removal, cleanup has\nhappened once `await Promise.resolve()` returns. If the element is inserted\nagain later, setup runs again.\n\n\n#### `ctx`\n\nThe argument passed to the callback given to `setup`.\n\n```ts\nctx:SetupContext<Props, RefsMap<RefDefs>, ContextsMap<CtxDefs>>\n```\n\n##### `ctx` Methods\n\n| Method | Returns | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `.peek(signal)` | `T` | Read a signal's current value without subscribing, even inside `ctx.effect`. |\n| `.on(target, type, handler, options?)` | `void` | Add a DOM event listener, auto-removed on teardown (a microtask after disconnect). |\n| `.emit(event)` / `.emit(name, detail?, options?)` | `boolean` | Dispatch a pre-built `Event` as-is, or construct and dispatch a bubbling `CustomEvent` from a name. Returns `dispatchEvent`'s result: `false` if a cancelable event had `preventDefault()` called. |\n| `.effect(fn)` | `void` | Run an alien-signals effect that auto-tracks the signals it reads. Auto-disposed on teardown. |\n| `.bind(signal, element, options?)` | `void` | Bind a signal to a DOM element property, auto-cleaned on teardown. |\n| `.onCleanup(fn)` | `void` | Register custom teardown, run a microtask after disconnect. |\n| `.consume(token)` | `T \\| undefined` | Read an optional context value published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n\nSee [Typed Events](#typed-events) for more about `.on` and `.emit`.\n\n##### `ctx.bind`\n\nThe `.bind` method treats the signal as the source of truth. The element\nproperty is set from the signal.\nWith no `options`, the control's element property and write-back event are\nauto-detected from its type:\n\n```ts\n{\n    bind<T> (\n        source:Signal<T>,\n        control:Element,\n        opts?:BindOptions\n    )\n}\n```\n\n| Control | `prop` | write-back `event` |\n|---------|--------|---------------------|\n| `<input type=\"checkbox\">` | `checked` | `change` |\n| `<input type=\"number\">` / `type=\"range\"` | `valueAsNumber` | `input` |\n| Other `<input>` types / `<textarea>` | `value` | `input` |\n| Anything else, e.g. `<select>` | `value` | `change` |\n\nPassing `options` overrides the auto-detected `prop`, and replaces the\nwrite-back `event` -- omit `options.event` for a one-way binding. A read-only\ngetter (`() => T`) is also accepted as the `signal` argument, for binding a\nderived value one-way (pass it with `options` and omit `event`).\n\n```ts\n// two-way, auto-detected (checkbox -> `checked` / `change`)\nctx.bind(checked, ctx.refs.toggle)\n\n// one-way: push a derived value into a read-only display\nctx.bind(() => `${count()} items`, ctx.refs.label, { prop: 'textContent' })\n```\n\n---\n\n### `bindProp`\n\nBind a signal (or computed) to a declared prop of another host, so the\nprop reads through to it. This is the same as assigning the signal to\nthe host's prop accessor, but typed: the signal's value type must match\nthe prop's type, and only declared props are accepted.\n\n```ts\nfunction bindProp (\n    host:HTMLElement,\n    name:string,  // a declared prop of `host`\n    source:() => T  // T is the prop's type\n):void\n```\n\n```ts\nimport { define, bindProp } from 'microtags'\nimport { computed } from 'alien-signals'\n\n// in a parent's setup, drive a nested host's `label` prop\nconst label = computed(() => String(ctx.props.count()))\nbindProp(ctx.refs.badge, 'label', label)\n```\n\nWhile bound, the prop is read-only inside the nested component, and the\nhost's HTML attribute is no longer updated. Assigning a plain value to\nthe accessor removes the binding.\n\n---\n\n\n## Missing refs\n\nAny calls to `r.one` in `.withRefs` will throw if there is not a matching\nchild element.\n\n```\nError: MY-COMPONENT: missing refs: button, save\n```\n\nThe tag name is upper case (it comes from `host.tagName`), and the\nmessage lists every required ref that was not found.\n\nCall to `r.all()` are **never reported**. A missing match resolves to an\nempty array, not an error.\n\nThis check happens right before the custom element runs your `.setup` function.\n\nFor a component with no contexts, refs are resolved at the moment the element\nconnects to the DOM. For a component that declares\n[`withContexts`](#withcontexts), ref collection is deferred along\nwith `setup` until every required context resolves.\n\n\n### Checking refs in tests\n\nA missing `r.one` ref throws while the component connects. When the browser\nruns a lifecycle callback (which is what `appendChild` triggers), it reports\nthe exception to the global `error` event rather than propagating it to the\ncaller, so a try / catch around `appendChild` sees nothing.\n\nYou need to listen for the global `error` event. When every\n`data-ref` the component declares is present, nothing is reported.\n\n```ts\nimport { test } from '@substrate-system/tapzero'\nimport './my-counter.js'  // the component from the example above\n\ntest('all refs resolve', t => {\n    let err:Error|null = null\n    const onError = (ev:ErrorEvent) => { err = ev.error }\n    window.addEventListener('error', onError)\n\n    const el = document.createElement('my-counter')\n    el.innerHTML = `\n        <div data-ref=\"display\"></div>\n        <button data-ref=\"dec\">-</button>\n        <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>`\n    document.body.appendChild(el)\n\n    t.ok(err === null, 'no missing refs')\n\n    window.removeEventListener('error', onError)\n    document.body.removeChild(el)\n})\n```\n\n\n## Typed Events\n\n`TypedEvent<Target, Detail>` is a type-only helper that narrows\n`CustomEvent` to a specific `target` and `detail`. Combine it with\n`HTMLElementEventMap` augmentation for app-wide type-safe events.\n\n```ts\nimport type { TypedEvent } from 'microtags'\n\ntype TabsChangedEvent = TypedEvent<InstanceType<typeof XTabs>, { index:number }>\n\ndeclare global {\n    interface HTMLElementEventMap {\n        'tabs:changed':TabsChangedEvent\n    }\n}\n\n// Emitting from setup:\nctx.emit('tabs:changed', { index: 2 })\n\n// Listening, fully typed:\nctx.on(tabsEl, 'tabs:changed', ev => {\n    ev.target   // the XTabs instance\n    ev.detail   // { index:number }\n})\n```\n\n## Templates\n\n[`render` and `renderList`](#microtagsrender) both take a `<template>`\nelement as their second argument. A\n[`<template>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/template)\nis a native HTML element that holds markup the browser parses but does not\nrender. Its contents are inert. Nothing inside paints, scripts do not run,\nimages and other resources do not load, and the nodes stay out of the\ndocument until you clone them.\n\nSee [./example/ssr.ts, line 89](./example/ssr.ts#L89).\n\nYou write the markup for an item inside the template, and the\nreconciler stamps out a copy per item by cloning `template.content`. The\ntemplate's first element child is the unit that gets cloned, so give each\ntemplate a single root element.\n\n```html\n<item-list>\n    <ul></ul>\n    <template>\n        <li>\n            <span class=\"name\"></span>\n            <span class=\"count\"></span>\n        </li>\n    </template>\n</item-list>\n```\n\nThe component resolves the list and the template as refs, then calls\n`renderList` from inside an effect so the list re-renders whenever the\ndata signal changes.\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { signal } from 'alien-signals'\nimport { renderList } from 'microtags/render'\n\ntype Item = { id:string; name:string; count:number }\n\ndefine('item-list')\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        list: r.one('ul'),\n        row: r.one('template'),  // typed as HTMLTemplateElement\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        const items = signal<Item[]>([\n            { id: 'a', name: 'Alice', count: 1 },\n            { id: 'b', name: 'Bob', count: 2 },\n        ])\n\n        // Re-runs on every items() change; only changed rows are touched.\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            renderList(ctx.refs.list, ctx.refs.row, {\n                data: items(),\n                key: item => item.id,\n                update: (el, item) => {\n                    el.querySelector('.name')!.textContent = item.name\n                    el.querySelector('.count')!.textContent =\n                        String(item.count)\n                },\n            })\n        })\n    })\n```\n\nBecause the template lives in your HTML instead of a JavaScript string, each\nrender clones real DOM nodes rather than re-parsing HTML, and the `update`\ncallback function mutates the DOM through properties like `textContent`.\nNothing concatenates strings into markup, so there is no string-injection\nXSS vector.\n\n\n## Subpath Exports\n\n### `microtags/context`\n\nCross-component context backed by alien-signals. A provider shares a value\nwith everything nested inside it; a consumer reads the value from its nearest\nprovider ancestor and reacts when it changes.\n\n`provide` seeds a signal the first time it runs and updates that same signal\non later calls, so re-providing inside an effect pushes changes to consumers.\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { signal } from 'alien-signals'\nimport { createContext, provide } from 'microtags/context'\n\nconst ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n\n// Provider: owns the theme and shares it with its descendants.\ndefine('theme-provider')\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        toggle: r.one('button'),\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        const theme = signal('light')\n\n        // Share with descendants; remove the entry on disconnect.\n        const cleanUp = provide(ctx.host, ThemeToken, theme())\n        ctx.onCleanup(cleanup)\n\n        // Re-provide on change. provide() updates the existing signal,\n        // so consumers that read this value re-run.\n        ctx.effect(() => provide(ctx.host, ThemeToken, theme()))\n\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.toggle, 'click', () => {\n            theme(theme() === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')\n        })\n    })\n\n// Consumer: reads the nearest provider's theme, reactively.\ndefine('themed-card')\n    .withContexts(() => ({\n        theme: ThemeToken,\n    }))\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // Reading ctx.contexts.theme inside an effect subscribes to it,\n        // so this re-runs whenever the provider toggles the value.\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            ctx.host.dataset.theme = ctx.contexts.theme\n        })\n    })\n```\n\n```html\n<theme-provider>\n    <button>Toggle theme</button>\n    <themed-card>Card A</themed-card>\n    <themed-card>Card B</themed-card>\n</theme-provider>\n```\n\nBoth cards resolve to the same nearest `<theme-provider>`, so one click\nupdates both. `resolveContextSignal(element, token)` is the low-level lookup\nthose reads use under the hood; reach for it only when you need the provider\nsignal imperatively.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/render`\n\n```ts\nimport { render, renderList } from 'microtags/render'\n```\n\nA keyed reconciler for rendering lists and single items from a\n`<template>`, with no virtual DOM and no diffing library.\n\nMicrotags' state is reactive. When a signal changes, you want the DOM to follow.\nA way to do that for a list is to rebuild the markup on every change,\ne.g. `container.innerHTML = items.map(toHTML).join('')`.\n\nBut rebuilding the markup on every change\n**throws away and recreates every node on every update**, losing any DOM\nstate, like focus, text selection, scroll position, and the internal state of\nelements like `<video>`, `<details>`, and `<iframe>`. It also re-parses HTML on\nevery pass, which creates a vector for string-concatenation XSS.\n\n**This library exports a `renderList` and a `render` function**.\nThey diff against the live DOM. See\n[example/render-demo.ts](./example/render-demo.ts) for a working example.\n\n#### `renderList`\n\nEach item is matched to an existing element by its `key` attribute, so nodes\nare reused across renders rather than being recreated. Nodes whose keys\ndisappear from the data are removed, surviving nodes are moved into place\nwith the fewest DOM operations, and your `update` callback runs only for\nitems whose value actually changed (compared by identity). Focus state and other\nDOM state survives a re-render, and changing one row touches only that row.\n\n```ts\nfunction renderList<T, E extends Element = Element> (\n    container:Element,\n    template:HTMLTemplateElement,\n    options:{\n        data:readonly T[];\n        key:(item:T, index:number) => string|number;\n        update:(el:E, item:T) => void;\n    }\n):void\n```\n\n##### `renderList` Example\n\nThe markup is the list container plus a `<template>` for one row:\n\n```html\n<ul></ul>\n<template>\n    <li>\n        <span class=\"name\"></span>\n    </li>\n</template>\n```\n\nCall `renderList` from inside an effect so it re-runs whenever the data\nsignal changes:\n\n```ts\nctx.effect(() => {\n    renderList(ctx.refs.list, ctx.refs.row, {\n        data: items(),\n        key: item => item.id,\n        update: (el, item) => {\n            el.querySelector('.name')!.textContent = item.name\n        },\n    })\n})\n```\n\n#### `render`\n\n`render` is the same machinery for a single item. It keys on the template, so\nrepeated calls update the same element in place instead of replacing it.\n\n```ts\nfunction render<T, E extends Element = Element> (\n    container:Element,\n    template:HTMLTemplateElement,\n    options?:{\n        data?:T;\n        update?:(el:E, item:T) => void;\n    }\n):void\n```\n\n##### `render` Example\n\nThe markup is a container plus a single-item `<template>`:\n\n```html\n<div class=\"item\"></div>\n<template>\n    <div>\n        <span></span>\n    </div>\n</template>\n```\n\n```ts\nimport { render } from 'microtags/render'\n\n// Render a single item into the container from the <template>.\nrender(container, template, {\n    data: item,\n    update: (el, item) => {\n        el.querySelector('span')!.textContent = item.name\n    },\n})\n```\n\nFor a runnable version, see `example/render-demo.ts`. It keeps an editable\n`<input>` in every row and a single item panel rendered with `render`, then\nre-renders on a timer and reorders on demand to show that focus, caret, and\ntyped text survive: keyed nodes are reused and at most moved, never rebuilt.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/util`\n\nHelpers for reading raw attribute strings and running cleanup\nfunctions. `withProps` uses these internally to coerce attribute values.\nThey are exported for when you need to read an attribute outside of the\nprop layer.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n    coerceNumber,\n    coerceString,\n    coerceBoolean,\n    coerceJson,\n    runAll,\n    toAttributes\n} from 'microtags/util'\n```\n\n#### Coercion\n\nEach coercion helper takes the raw `getAttribute` result -- a `string` or\n`null` -- and returns a typed value. None of them throw.\n\n```ts\nfunction coerceNumber (raw:string | null):number\nfunction coerceString (raw:string | null):string\nfunction coerceBoolean (raw:string | null):boolean\nfunction coerceJson<T = unknown> (raw:string | null):T | undefined\n```\n\n- `coerceNumber` -- `Number(raw)`, or `NaN` when the attribute is absent\n  or cannot be parsed.\n- `coerceString` -- the value as-is; a missing attribute (`null`) becomes\n  the empty string `''`.\n- `coerceBoolean` -- presence-based, matching the HTML boolean-attribute\n  convention: absent (`null`) is `false`, present is `true`, including the\n  empty string from `disabled=\"\"`.\n- `coerceJson` -- `JSON.parse(raw)`, or `undefined` when the attribute is\n  absent or not valid JSON. Pass a type parameter to annotate the result.\n\n#### `runAll`\n\nRuns every function in the array, even if some throw. After all have run\nit re-throws the first error encountered, so one failing teardown cannot\nskip the rest. A `Cleanup` is a no-argument teardown function, `() => void`.\n\n```ts\nfunction runAll (fns:Cleanup[]):void\n```\n\n#### `toAttributes`\n\nTransform an object into an HTML attributes string. The object should be\nlike `{ attributeName: value }`. Handles boolean values by adding a string, eg\n`{ disabled: true }` = `<button disabled>`. Arrays are converted to a list in\nquotes, eg `{ class: ['abc', 'def'] }` = `<div class=\"abc def\">`\n\n```ts\ntype Attrs = Record<\n    string,\n    undefined|null|string|number|boolean|(string|number)[]\n>\n\n/**\n * Transform an object into an HTML attributes string. The object should be\n * like `{ attributeName: value }`.\n *\n * @param {Attrs} attrs An object for the attributes.\n * @returns {string} A string suitable for use as HTML attributes.\n */\nfunction toAttributes (attrs:Attrs):string\n```\n\n### `microtags/htm`\n\nA tagged-template string renderer that will expand any microtags\ninstances if finds. Exports `html`, `h`, `raw`, and the `Markup` type.\n`html`, `h`, and `raw` return `Markup`, not a string. Convert it with\n`String(m)` where a string is required, for example `innerHTML`. In the\nbrowser the same import resolves to a build that also exports `mount`;\nsee [ADR-007](./docs/adr/ADR-007-client-templater.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { html, raw } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { MyCounter } from './components.js'\n\nconst page = html`<div>\n    <${MyCounter} start=${5}>\n        <p>Extra children</p>\n    </${MyCounter}>\n    ${raw('<b>trusted markup</b>')}\n</div>`\n```\n\nWhen `html` meets a microtags element class, it splits props from\npassthrough attributes using the class's static `props` list, joins\nchildren into one string, and calls the static `render`. Plain string\ntag names emit ordinary HTML.\n\n**Escaping.** Every interpolated string is escaped (`<`, `>`, `&`, `\"`,\n`'`) before the parser sees it. Component output and `raw()` output\nare trusted and never re-escaped. Static template text is trusted.\n\n**Arrays.** Array children (from `.map()`) are flattened automatically.\n\n**Void elements.** Write void tags in the self-closing form, like\n`<br />`, `<img src=\"x\" />`, and `<input value=${v} />`. The output has\nno closing tag and no trailing slash, eg `<br>`. The `htm` parser does\nnot know which tags are void, so an unclosed `<br>` would be treated\nas an open tag and swallow the content after it. Instead of dropping\nthat content, the templater throws, on the server and in the browser,\nand the error names the tag and its self-closing form.\n\n**Custom elements.** A component tag always gets a closing tag in the\noutput, eg `<my-counter start=\"5\">...</my-counter>`, because HTML has no\nself-closing custom elements. In a template, close a component with\n`</${MyComponent}>` or with the `htm` shorthand `<//>`, which closes\nthe nearest open tag without repeating its name.\n\n#### What escaping does not cover\n\nEscaping keeps an interpolated string from becoming markup. It does not\nmake every value safe in every place it can go.\n\n**URL schemes.** Attribute escaping keeps a value inside its quotes,\nbut `href=${url}` with `url` set to `javascript:alert(1)` is still a\nworking link. The same goes for `src`, `action`, `formaction`, and\nother attributes that take a URL. Check the scheme yourself (for\nexample, allow only `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:`) before a URL from\nuser data reaches the template.\n\n**`<script>` and `<style>`.** HTML escaping is the wrong encoding inside\nthese elements. The browser does not decode entities there, so an\nescaped value comes out mangled, and HTML escaping does not stop a\nvalue from breaking out of a JavaScript string or a CSS rule. Do not\ninterpolate user data into them. To pass data to a script, serialize it\nas JSON in a `<script type=\"application/json\">` and escape `<` in the\nJSON, the way the hydration example's state script does.\n\n**`raw`.** `raw()` skips escaping by construction. Pass it only markup\nyou wrote or trust, never user data.\n\n**Attribute names.** Escaping covers values, not names. A name must\nstart with a letter, `_` or `:`, followed by letters, digits, `-`,\n`_`, `:` or `.`. Any other name, for example a key from a spread of\nuser data such as `x\" onload=\"...`, is skipped without an error, on the\nserver, in the browser, and on a component's host tag. Valid keys in the\nsame spread are still written.\n\n**Void elements.** A void element with children throws instead of\nrendering, on the server and in the browser. Write void tags in the\nself-closing form, `<input />`, so an unclosed tag cannot swallow the\nmarkup after it.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/render-support`\n\nThe host-tag builder, prop serializers and attribute escaping that a\ncomponent's static `render` and `renderParts` use. Import it for its\nside effect:\n\n```ts\nimport 'microtags/render-support'\n```\n\nIt is separate from core so that a client bundle with every\n`.render` link stripped leaves this code out. The `microtags/htm`\ntemplater imports it already, and the [`microtags/build`](#microtagsbuild)\ntransform adds it to any module where it keeps a `.render` link, so\nonly code that calls a `render` or `renderParts` static without\neither needs this import. See\n[ADR-009](./docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md).\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`\n\nNode-only. Strips [`.render()`](#builderrenderfn-options) calls from\ndefine chains so the client bundle contains only hydration code.\nRequires `typescript` as a peer dependency.\n\n```ts\nimport { transform } from 'microtags/build'\n\nconst result = transform(source, {\n    keep: ['my-notice'],   // keep these tags' render in the bundle\n    cascade: true,         // dead-import removal, default false\n    filename: 'app.ts',    // shown in warnings\n})\n\nconsole.log(result.code)\nfor (const w of result.warnings) {\n    console.warn(`${w.file}:${w.line} ${w.message}`)\n}\n```\n\n**Strip rule:** a `.render(...)` call is removed when it is a link in a\ncall chain rooted at a `define(...)` call imported from `microtags`,\nwith import aliases tracked. Nothing else named `render` is touched.\n\n**Keep rule (union):** the call is kept when its options argument is an\nobject literal containing `client: true`, or when the string literal\npassed to `define` is listed in `keep`.\n\n**Output fidelity:** removed spans are replaced with the same number of\nnewlines. Every other byte is unchanged, apart from the support import\nbelow. TS in, TS out.\n\n**Support import:** when a module keeps at least one `.render` link,\nthe transform appends `import 'microtags/render-support'` after the\nlast line, unless the module already imports it, so kept components\ncan render in the browser. A module where every link was stripped gets\nno such import, and a bundle with no kept link and no templater import\ncontains none of the server render code. A bundler config that aliases\n`microtags` to source should alias `microtags/render-support` too.\n\n**Unsupported:** `const b = define('x'); b.render(fn)` -- the\nnon-chained form is not matched and is left unchanged.\n\n**`client: true` detection:** only an object literal is recognised. A\nvariable or spread in the options position is not.\n\n**Type gap:** `MicrotagElementClass` declares `render` and `props` as\nalways present. After stripping, `render` is `undefined` at runtime.\n\n**Warnings** (each with file and line):\n\n| Kind | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| `outside-chain` | `.render(` call not in a define chain |\n| `stripped-static` | `X.render(` after X's render was stripped |\n| `non-literal-tag` | `define(variable)` with `keep` set |\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/esbuild`\n\nNode-only esbuild plugin that calls [`transform`](#microtagsbuild) on\nevery matching file during the build.\n\n```ts\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/esbuild'\n\n// in your esbuild config\nplugins: [\n    microtags({\n        cascade: true,\n        keep: ['my-notice'],\n    }),\n]\n```\n\nThe plugin accepts the same options as `transform` plus a file `filter`.\nWarnings are forwarded to esbuild's warning channel.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/vite`\n\nNode-only Vite plugin, same interface as the\n[esbuild plugin](#microtagsesbuild).\n\n```ts\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/vite'\n\n// in vite.config.ts\nplugins: [\n    microtags({\n        cascade: true,\n        keep: ['my-notice'],\n    }),\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n### `microbuild`\n\nCLI for stripping `.render()` calls outside a bundler.\n\n```sh\n# stdin/stdout -- one file\ncat src/counter.ts | microbuild --cascade > dist/counter.ts\n\n# glob mode -- requires --outdir\nmicrobuild 'src/**/*.ts' --outdir dist --cascade --keep my-notice\n```\n\n`--keep` is repeatable and accepts comma-separated tag names. `--root`\nsets the base for output paths (defaults to the current directory).\nFiles are never rewritten in place.\n\nstdin mode transforms one file only. A multi-file app should use the\n[esbuild](#microtagsesbuild) or [vite](#microtagsvite) plugin, or\nglob mode.\n\n---\n\n### `client:true` vs `--keep`\n\nBoth prevent [`microbuild`](#microbuild) from stripping a component's\n`.render()` call, but they work at different levels:\n\n- **`{ client: true }`** is set in source, in the `.render()` options.\n  Use it for components that must render themselves in the browser --\n  for example, a notice inserted dynamically after page load. The\n  decision travels with the component and applies in every build.\n\n- **`--keep tag-name`** (or the `keep` option in the plugin) is set at\n  build time. Use it to override stripping for specific tags without\n  changing source, for example during development or in a build\n  variant that needs client rendering.\n\nBoth can be combined. A component is kept when either condition is met\n(union semantics).\n\n---\n\n## Differences from `nanotags`\n\n### Reactivity primitive\n\n`nanotags` uses `nanostores` atoms; `microtags` uses `alien-signals`.\n\n| nanotags (nanostores) | microtags (alien-signals) |\n|---|---|\n| `$count = atom(0)` | `const count = signal(0)` |\n| `$count.get()` | `count()` |\n| `$count.set(1)` | `count(1)` |\n| `ctx.props.$count` (store) | `ctx.props.count` (callable signal) |\n\nProps declared with `withProps` are signals. There is no `$` prefix convention;\nall prop names are plain identifiers.\n\n### Effects + Subscriptions\n\nIn `nanotags`, `ctx.effect` takes the store(s) to watch\nexplicitly and hands the value to the callback:\n\n```ts\n// nanotags\nctx.effect(ctx.props.$count, count => {\n    ctx.refs.display.textContent = String(count)\n})\n```\n\nIn `microtags`, `ctx.effect` takes a zero-argument function and tracks whichever\nsignals are read inside it (the `alien-signals` model):\n\n```ts\n// microtags\nctx.effect(() => {\n    ctx.refs.display.textContent = String(ctx.props.count())\n})\n```\n\nThe read operation in `ctx.effect` subscribes to the signal, and runs on\nany signal change. A read inside an effect that you do *not* want to track has\nto go through [`peek` or `untracked`](#peek-and-untracked).\nIn nanotags `.get()` never subscribes, so there is no equivalent.\n\n### Refs: `r.all` instead of `r.many`\n\nThe multi-element ref is `r.all()` in `microtags` and `r.many()` in `nanotags`.\nThe empty-match behavior also differs: `r.all()` returns an empty array when\nnothing matches, whereas nanotags' `r.many()` throws. `r.one()` throws on a\nmissing element in both.\n\n\n### Context API\n\nThe context API is shaped differently. See\n[`microtags/context`](#microtagscontext).\n\n| nanotags | microtags |\n|---|---|\n| `createContext<T>('name')` | `createContext<T>()`, no name |\n| `token.provide(ctx, value)` | `provide(ctx.host, token, value)`, standalone, returns a cleanup |\n| `withContexts({ theme: token })` | `withContexts(() => ({ theme: token }))`, a function |\n| `token.consume(ctx, cb)` | `ctx.consume(token)`, returns value-or-`undefined` |\n\n### Props\n\n| nanotags | microtags |\n|---|---|\n| `p.json(schema, default)`, hydrated from a `<script type=\"application/json\">` tag, not an attribute | `p.json<T>()`, type-only, parsed from the observed attribute |\n| custom schema passed directly as the prop def | wrap it in `p.schema(validator)` |\n| property-only via `{ schema, attribute: false }` | `p.prop(initial)` |\n| nullable fallback `p.string(null)` | no null-fallback argument; a failed `p.schema` falls back to `undefined` |\n\n### Not ported\n\nThese nanotags APIs have no microtags equivalent:\n\n- `ctx.getElement` / `ctx.getElements` -- declare refs with `withRefs`, or\n  call `ctx.host.querySelector` directly.\n- The `define(name, setupFn)` two-argument shorthand -- always use the\n  builder chain ending in `.setup()`.\n\n### Additions / New Features\n\n* Serverside-compatible API -- see [serverside example](#serverside-example) --\n  we expose `.TAG` and `.refs` on your component, which can be imported in a\n  server environment.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1508,"oldLines":6,"newStart":1508,"newLines":100,"lines":[" "," ---"," ","+### `microtags/preact`","+","+Use a microtags component inside a Preact app, rendered on the server","+and hydrated in the browser, or rendered client-only. `toPreact(El,","+{ events })` takes an element class and returns a Preact component that","+renders the host tag. Preact owns the host and its attributes, and the","+element owns the host's children. See","+[ADR-010](./docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md).","+","+```ts","+import { toPreact } from 'microtags/preact'","+import { NumberInput } from './number-input.js'","+","+// callback prop name -> DOM event name","+export const PreactNumberInput = toPreact(NumberInput, {","+    events: { onChange: 'number-input:change' },","+})","+```","+","+The component takes the element's declared props (typed from the","+class), one callback prop per entry in `events`, and `class`, `id`,","+`aria-*` and `data-*` props, which pass through to the host.","+","+**Server render, then hydrate.** On the server, the adapter fills the","+host with the component's own markup, so `renderToString` gives the","+same HTML as the component's `render` static. Server code must import","+a variant of the component that keeps its `.render` link; a class with","+no render link throws an error naming its tag.","+","+```ts","+// server","+import { h } from 'preact'","+import { renderToString } from 'preact-render-to-string'","+","+const body = renderToString(h(App, null))","+```","+","+```ts","+// browser","+import { h, hydrate } from 'preact'","+","+hydrate(h(App, null), document.getElementById('root')!)","+```","+","+In `App`, use the component like any other:","+","+```ts","+h(PreactNumberInput, {","+    min: 1,","+    class: 'qty',","+    onChange: (ev:CustomEvent) => setQty(ev.detail),","+})","+```","+","+In the browser, the host gets a fixed, empty `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.","+While hydrating, Preact leaves that element's children alone, so the","+element adopts the server markup. Because the value never changes,","+later renders never write `innerHTML` either.","+","+**Client-only render.** With no server markup, call Preact's `render`.","+The first render empties the host once, and the element renders itself","+when it connects, so the browser bundle needs the variant of the","+component that keeps its render link.","+","+```ts","+import { h, render } from 'preact'","+","+render(h(PreactNumberInput, { min: 1 }), document.body)","+```","+","+**Two builds.** The import resolves by export condition, the same way","+as [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm). The `browser` condition selects a","+build that contains no render code. The default build, for Node and","+SSR, imports [`microtags/render-support`](#microtagsrender-support).","+","+**Peer dependency.** `preact` is an optional peer dependency. Install","+it yourself. Only `microtags/preact` imports it.","+","+**No children.** The adapter does not accept Preact children. Passing","+them is a type error, and the adapter throws.","+","+**Props before definition.** If the element is not defined yet when","+Preact first sets a prop, the element has no property by that name, so","+Preact sets an attribute instead. The component reads its declared","+props from attributes when it connects, so string-valued props still","+arrive. The adapter does not work around this.","+","+**Other host renderers.** The adapter is built on the","+[`renderParts`](#builderrenderfn-options) static, which returns the","+host tag, its serialized attributes and its inner HTML separately. An","+adapter for another framework can build on the same parts.","+","+---","+"," ### `microtags/build`"," "," Node-only. Strips [`.render()`](#builderrenderfn-options) calls from"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SgcoYKfp18UPKZ9AmyibDa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
